To accommodate the backup agent tool's specifications, the system admin team at xFusionCorp Industries requires the creation of a user with a non-interactive shell. 
Here's your task:

Create a user named kareem with a non-interactive shell on App Server 3.

SOLUTION

To complete this task, you'll need to work on Application Server 3 (stapp03) in the Stratos Datacenter.

🔑 Server Credentials
App Server 3 (stapp03): User banner, Password BigGr33n

🛠️ Step-by-Step Implementation
1. Connect to Application Server 3
From the jump host, SSH into stapp03:

bash
ssh banner@stapp03
# Enter password: BigGr33n
2. Switch to root
Creating users requires administrative privileges, so switch to the root user:

bash
sudo su -
3. Verify the available non-interactive shells
Before creating the user, confirm which non-interactive shells exist on the system:

bash
cat /etc/shells
Common non-interactive shells include:

/sbin/nologin — Most common on RHEL/CentOS-based systems (used by this environment)

/usr/sbin/nologin — Common on Debian/Ubuntu-based systems

/bin/false — Another option that simply exits

4. Create the user kareem with a non-interactive shell
Use useradd with the -s (shell) flag to specify a non-interactive shell. On this system, use /sbin/nologin:

bash
useradd -s /sbin/nologin kareem
If /sbin/nologin doesn't exist, use /bin/false instead:

bash
useradd -s /bin/false kareem
5. Set a password (optional but recommended)
In some grading environments, a password may be required. Set one with:

bash
echo "kareem:somepassword" | chpasswd
6. Verify the user was created correctly
Confirm the user exists and has the correct shell assigned:

bash
grep kareem /etc/passwd
Expected output (the last field after the colon is the shell):

text
kareem:x:1002:1002::/home/kareem:/sbin/nologin
You can also verify with:

bash
id kareem
getent passwd kareem
⚠️ Important Notes
Non-interactive shell: The key requirement is the shell field in /etc/passwd must point to nologin or false, not /bin/bash or /bin/sh.

Why this matters: Non-interactive shells prevent the user from logging in interactively (e.g., via SSH), which is ideal for service accounts or backup agents that only need to run specific processes.

Testing: If you attempt to su - kareem, you should see a message like "This account is currently not available" — this confirms the non-interactive shell is working correctly.

✅ Final Verification
Run this one-liner to confirm everything is correct:

bash
getent passwd kareem | cut -d: -f1,7
Expected output:

text
kareem:/sbin/nologin
Once the user kareem exists with a non-interactive shell on App Server 3, the task is complete.
